WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.920
Three diseases each kill a particular set of adult neurons.

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:10.520
How do we learn what causes them, and what might treat them?

3
00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:17.790
In Alzheimer disease, early losses include neurons of the entorhinal cortex, and of

4
00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.490
field CA1 in the hippocampus.

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.940
Both belong to the circuit for new memories.

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.120
In Parkinson disease, the defining loss is of dopamine neurons in the substantia

7
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.460
nigra, in the midbrain.

8
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.500
In ALS, motor neurons die.

9
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.060
Upper ones sit in the motor cortex.

10
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.700
Lower ones send axons to muscle.

11
00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:42.633
In each disease, a protein misfolds and clumps.

12
00:00:43.533 --> 00:00:48.353
Alzheimer disease has amyloid plaques outside neurons, and tau tangles inside them.

13
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:55.007
In Parkinson disease, α-synuclein is the main protein in the filaments of Lewy

14
00:00:55.007 --> 00:00:58.667
bodies. Most ALS has clumps of TDP-43.

15
00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:02.967
Now three questions, each with its own kind of evidence.

16
00:01:03.987 --> 00:01:06.647
First, an autopsy tells you what a clump is made of.

17
00:01:07.407 --> 00:01:09.867
It does not tell you whether the clump kills the cell.

18
00:01:10.817 --> 00:01:12.317
Now look at rare families.

19
00:01:13.257 --> 00:01:16.677
A gene variant passes from parent to child along with the illness.

20
00:01:17.717 --> 00:01:20.537
That travelling together is called cosegregation.

21
00:01:21.357 --> 00:01:23.017
It is strong evidence for a cause.

22
00:01:23.657 --> 00:01:26.697
But a harmless variant nearby can travel along too.

23
00:01:27.597 --> 00:01:31.077
A second family, and tests of what the variant does, make the case.

24
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.620
Here is a puzzle. A family's ALS is caused by a mutation in SOD1.

25
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.600
Most people with ALS carry TDP-43 clumps.

26
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:44.420
Will this family's motor neurons have them?

27
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:57.973
No. In a postmortem series, cases with SOD1 mutations lacked TDP-43 clumps.

28
00:01:58.793 --> 00:02:00.253
Their motor neurons died anyway.

29
00:02:02.816 --> 00:02:07.116
A disease-causing gene need not encode the protein in the usual deposit.

30
00:02:08.016 --> 00:02:12.816
In Alzheimer disease, the three classic dominant genes all affect how amyloid-beta

31
00:02:12.816 --> 00:02:14.156
is made or processed.

32
00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:21.070
Neither deposits nor disease-causing genes alone show that removing a protein after

33
00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:26.550
onset will help. Randomized trials in people give the most direct test, judged on

34
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.530
how they think, function or survive.

35
00:02:29.583 --> 00:02:31.583
So ask what each finding can show.

36
00:02:32.583 --> 00:02:34.123
Autopsy shows what builds up.

37
00:02:35.143 --> 00:02:36.483
Families can show what starts it.

38
00:02:37.443 --> 00:02:39.883
Trials test whether changing it helps, and what it costs.

39
00:02:45.916 --> 00:02:47.716
Ask two things of every finding.

40
00:02:48.656 --> 00:02:49.656
What does it show?

41
00:02:50.176 --> 00:02:51.156
And what can it not show?
